	url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780081005965230199},
	abstract = {The cow's mammary gland is attached to the abdomen by ligaments and the epithelium is composed of alveoli that convert blood components into milk, which exits through the teat canal. Nutrients enter via the mammary artery, ending in capillaries that supply milk precursors. Small veins exit alveoli and merge together to run dorsally, becoming the mammary veins. Interstitial fluids recirculate via the lymphatic system, removing waste products. Sensory nerves carry impulses from the udder to the brain. The nervous system is essential for milk let-down by triggering hormone release from the brain to alveoli.},
